# CHAPTER IX

## IMPLEMENTATION OF ASSISTANCE

#### Article 32

1. Assistance is carried out by persons or institutions that have expertise in conducting counseling, therapy, and advocacy for the strengthening and recovery of victims of violence;
2. Assistance carried out by persons or institutions as referred to in paragraph (1) must be coordinated with P2TP2A.

# CHAPTER X

## HANDLING OF CHILDREN IN CONFLICT WITH THE LAW

#### Article 33

Handling of children in conflict with the law includes:
a. children as perpetrators;
b. children as victims; and
c. children as witnesses to criminal acts.

#### Article 34

Treatment of children as perpetrators of criminal acts as referred to in Article 33 letter a, is as follows:
a. investigators undertake efforts to handle cases of children in conflict with the law with a restorative justice approach for the best interests of the child and must involve the Correctional Center, parents and/or families of victims and perpetrators of criminal acts as well as local community leaders;
b. the correctional center is obliged to prepare a correctional research report;
c. in the event that a child is detained, their placement is separated from adult detainees or placed in a detention center specifically for children;
d. the investigation process and submission of case files to the Public Prosecutor is carried out immediately by involving the Community Supervisor of the Correctional Center;
e. in the event that the Public Prosecutor is of the opinion that from the results of the investigation and the results of community research prosecution can be carried out by means of a restorative justice approach, then the Public Prosecutor immediately submits the case file to the District Court;
f. after receiving the submission from the Public Prosecutor, the Judge immediately conducts a child trial with a restorative justice approach procedure;
g. if the court decision is in the form of an action, then the Correctional Center is obliged to conduct guidance and supervision;
h. guidance, coaching and care at the Correctional Center, Detention Center and Correctional Institution is carried out in an integrated manner involving relevant agencies; and
i. in the event that the court decision hands over the child in conflict with the law to the Social Service, then the Social Service is obliged to receive and prepare facilities and infrastructure in the context of recovery and social rehabilitation of the child.

#### Article 35

Treatment of children as victims of criminal acts as referred to in Article 33 letter b, is as follows:
a. immediately after receiving a report from the victim of a criminal act, the investigator conducts an initial interview;
b. in conducting the interview, the investigator pays attention to the situation and condition of the victim;
c. if the victim requires further treatment, after the initial interview the victim may be treated or placed in a special room (temporarily) for other necessary services such as medical care, psychology or examination in the context of the investigation and inquiry process; and
d. after the victim has recovered and all services including the legal process are completed, the victim may be returned home or placed in a child social protection home, safe house, trauma center, for social and mental rehabilitation.

#### Article 36

Treatment of children as witnesses to criminal acts as referred to in Article 33 letter c, is as follows:
a. in the event that a child reports as a witness, then the police immediately contact the child's parents or guardian, unless the child's parents or guardian are involved or suspected as perpetrators;
b. make a record of the identity of the referring party, data regarding the child, chronology of events;
c. request the parent or guardian trusted by the child, to accompany the child when the child gives testimony;
d. in the event that a child gives testimony in the investigation process, it is conducted in a special and closed room accompanied by parents/guardians taking into account the age, intellectual level, and importance of the child's testimony;
e. the child has the right to receive protection from witness and victim protection institutions in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
f. if medical treatment is required, the child is referred to the nearest hospital;
g. at every stage of examination up to examination in court, the child as a witness must be provided with an assistant.

# CHAPTER XI

## COOPERATION AND PARTNERSHIP

## Part One

Cooperation

#### Article 37

1. In order to achieve the objectives of protection of women and children from acts of violence as referred to in Article 3, the Regional Government cooperates with:
a. the Government;
b. the Provincial Government;
c. other Regency/City Governments; and/or
d. non-governmental institutions.
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) is followed up in accordance with applicable law, including:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. provision of evidence and witnesses;
c. rehabilitation of victims of acts of violence; and
d. repatriation and social reintegration.
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) is set forth in the form of a Joint Agreement.

## Part Two

Partnership

#### Article 38

1. The Regional Government establishes partnerships with the business world in the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
2. Partnership as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out through:
a. notification of information on employment opportunities for women victims of acts of violence;
b. education and training for women victims of acts of violence;
c. educational assistance for children victims of acts of violence; and
d. fostering and increasing the economic independence of women victims of acts of violence.
4. Partnership as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) is set forth in the form of an agreement.

# CHAPTER XII

## FUNDING

#### Article 39

1. Funds for the implementation of protection of women and children victims of acts of violence managed by P2TP2A must be allocated in the Regional Revenue and Expenditure Budget;
2. In addition to the budget sources as referred to in paragraph (1), funds for the implementation of protection of women and children victims of acts of violence may also be sourced from other legitimate and non-binding budgets.

# CHAPTER XIII

## GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

#### Article 40

1. The Regional Government is obliged to conduct guidance and supervision of the implementation of protection of women and children victims of acts of violence;
2. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. service guidelines and standards;
b. technical guidance and training;
c. provision of facilities;
d. monitoring; and
e. evaluation.
3. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (2) in order to realize the objectives of protection of women and children victims of acts of violence in accordance with minimum service standards implemented by SKPD/P2TP2A and the community.

#### Article 41

1. The Regional Government is obliged to conduct supervision of the implementation of protection of women and children victims of acts of violence;
2. Supervision as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out with the principles of professionalism, transparency and accountability.

#### Article 42

The community may conduct supervision of the implementation of protection of women and children victims of acts of violence organized by the Regional Government.

# CHAPTER XIV

## CLOSING

#### Article 43

This Regional Regulation comes into force on the date of promulgation.

